    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The counts palatine of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> ruled scattered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland">Rhineland</a> lands from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a> and were imperial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> from 1356.</li><li>It was the leading Reformed Protestant state of the Empire and the home of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg-catechism">Heidelberg Catechism</a> of 1563.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-v-of-the-palatinate">Frederick V</a>'s acceptance of the Bohemian crown in 1619 began its ruin; in 1803 its last lands were divided among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> and others.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a>'s great library, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bibliotheca-palatina">Bibliotheca Palatina</a>, was sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 1623 as a gift to the pope after the city fell, and it remains in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vatican-library">Vatican Library</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

