    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/henry-of-laach"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/henry-of-laach.jpg" alt="Henry of Laach" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/henry-of-laach">Henry of Laach</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grabmal_Heinrich_II_von_Laach_Abtei_Maria_Laach_2011.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Holger Weinandt, CC BY-SA 3.0 de</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The office of count palatine began as that of the king's deputy in a region, presiding over justice in his name. In Lotharingia it was held in the eleventh century by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ezzonids">Ezzonid</a> family, with lands around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen">Aachen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>, but pressure from the archbishops of Cologne pushed the counts palatine southwards to the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>. Around 1085–1087 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-of-laach">Henry of Laach</a> is first called count palatine of the Rhine, the title the office kept thereafter. In 1156 Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a> gave the palatinate to his half-brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conrad-count-palatine-of-the-rhine">Conrad of Hohenstaufen</a>, who built a territorial base on the Rhine and the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neckar">Neckar</a> around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alzey">Alzey</a>, together with advocacies over church lands. Through Conrad's daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agnes-of-merania">Agnes</a> it passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-welf">Welf</a> Henry of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a>, and in 1214 Emperor Frederick II granted it to Louis I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> of the house of Wittelsbach, whose son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-ii-duke-of-bavaria">Otto II</a> married the Welf heiress. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wittelsbach">Wittelsbachs</a> held it until 1803. By the Treaty of Pavia of 1329 Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-iv-holy-roman-emperor">Louis IV the Bavarian</a> divided the family lands, giving the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palatinate-region">Rhenish Palatinate</a> and the Upper Palatinate north of Regensburg to his brother's sons <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudolf-ii-holy-roman-emperor">Rudolf II</a> and Rupert I, while Bavaria stayed with his own line. Heidelberg, first mentioned in 1196, became the residence of the counts palatine.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/bacharach"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bacharach.jpg" alt="Bacharach" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/bacharach">Bacharach</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bacharach_from_the_Postenturm.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jiuguang Wang, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Electoral Palatinate was a fragmented territory of scattered districts on both banks of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hunsruck">Hunsrück</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odenwald">Odenwald</a>, interspersed with bishoprics, imperial cities and lesser counties. Its wealth came from fertile farmland, the vineyards of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haardt">Haardt</a> and the Rhine valley, and especially from the Rhine tolls collected at places such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bacharach">Bacharach</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaub">Kaub</a>, where the island toll castle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pfalzgrafenstein-castle">Pfalzgrafenstein</a> still stands, which made the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> among the richest princes of the Empire in the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a> was the cultural centre: Rupert I founded its university in 1386, the third in the Empire after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, and the electors' library, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bibliotheca-palatina">Bibliotheca Palatina</a>, became one of the finest collections in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> in the sixteenth century. Religion shaped society deeply. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-henry-elector-palatine">Otto Henry</a> introduced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a> in 1556, and Frederick III turned to Reformed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestantism">Protestantism</a>, ordering images removed from churches and imposing strict church discipline. The Palatinate welcomed Calvinist refugees from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, who founded settlements such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankenthal">Frankenthal</a>, and Frederick IV founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mannheim">Mannheim</a> as a fortified town in 1606. Repeated wars in the seventeenth century devastated the land, and in the eighteenth century poverty and religious pressure drove thousands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-palatines">Palatines</a> to emigrate, many to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, where they formed a large part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania-dutch">Pennsylvania Dutch</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-seckenheim"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-seckenheim.jpg" alt="Battle of Seckenheim" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-seckenheim">Battle of Seckenheim</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Memorial_Battle_of_Seckenheim1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MSeses, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Palatinate was never a major military power, but its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> played a large part in the Empire's wars. Frederick I the Victorious (r. 1449–1476), who took power as guardian of his nephew Philip, defeated a coalition led by the margrave of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a>, the count of Württemberg and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-seckenheim">Battle of Seckenheim</a> in 1462, capturing all three. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-landshut-succession">Landshut War of Succession</a> (1503–1505), fought over the claims of Elector Philip's son Rupert to the Bavarian inheritance, ended in defeat, the loss of lands to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> and Württemberg and the creation of the smaller <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palatinate-neuburg">Palatinate-Neuburg</a> for Rupert's sons. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> era the Calvinist electors became leaders of militant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestantism">Protestantism</a>: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-casimir-of-the-palatinate-simmern">John Casimir</a> led armies to aid the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huguenots">Huguenots</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and Frederick IV headed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-union">Protestant Union</a> founded in 1608. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-v-of-the-palatinate">Frederick V</a>'s acceptance of the Bohemian crown in 1619 brought catastrophe. His army was destroyed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-white-mountain">Battle of White Mountain</a> on 8 November 1620, Spanish troops under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambrogio-spinola">Spinola</a> occupied the left bank, and in 1622 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-league">Catholic League</a>'s army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-tserclaes-count-of-tilly">Tilly</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a>. The Palatinate suffered again in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nine-years-war">Nine Years' War</a>, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>'s troops, pressing the inheritance claims of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-charlotte-madame-palatine">Elizabeth Charlotte</a>, duchess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orleans">Orléans</a>, systematically burned Heidelberg, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mannheim">Mannheim</a>, Speyer and Worms in 1689, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg-castle">Heidelberg Castle</a> was blown up in 1689 and 1693.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/golden-bull-of-1356"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/golden-bull-of-1356.jpg" alt="Golden Bull of 1356" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/golden-bull-of-1356">Golden Bull of 1356</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Goldene_Bulle_Nahaufnahme.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Karl IV., Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-bull-of-1356">Golden Bull of 1356</a> confirmed the count palatine of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> as one of the seven <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> who chose the King of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, with the office of arch-steward of the Empire and the right to act as imperial vicar in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland">Rhineland</a>, Swabia and the lands of Franconian law when the throne was vacant. Elector <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rupert-of-germany">Rupert III</a> was himself elected King of the Romans in 1400, after the deposition of Wenceslaus, and reigned until 1410. On his death the lands were divided among his four sons, creating the electoral line and the branches of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neumarkt-in-der-oberpfalz">Neumarkt</a>, Simmern-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zweibrucken">Zweibrücken</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosbach">Mosbach</a>; later extinctions repeatedly brought the electorate to collateral lines, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simmern">Simmern</a> in 1559, the Catholic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neuburg-an-der-donau">Neuburg</a> in 1685 and the Sulzbach in 1742. The electors governed through a council in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a> and district officials in the <em>Oberämter</em>, and unlike many German princes they faced no powerful territorial estates. Frederick III created a church council to run the Reformed church and sponsored the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg-catechism">Heidelberg Catechism</a> of 1563, chiefly the work of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zacharias-ursinus">Zacharias Ursinus</a>, and in 1582 the territory received a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/common-law">common law</a> code, the <em>Landrecht</em>. After the Thirty Years' War Charles I Louis rebuilt the administration, and in 1720 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iii-philip-elector-palatine">Charles III Philip</a> moved the residence to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mannheim">Mannheim</a>, laid out on a grid around a vast new palace.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg-catechism"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/heidelberg-catechism.jpg" alt="Heidelberg Catechism" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg-catechism">Heidelberg Catechism</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Heidelberger_Katechismus_1563_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Palatinate's main legacy lay in religion, learning and the politics of the Empire. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg-catechism">Heidelberg Catechism</a> became one of the most widely used confessions of the Reformed churches, approved by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/synod-of-dort">Synod of Dort</a> in 1618–1619 and carried to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> and elsewhere, and it remains in use today. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg-university">University of Heidelberg</a> is the oldest university in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. The marriage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-v-of-the-palatinate">Frederick V</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-stuart-queen-of-bohemia">Elizabeth Stuart</a>, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-vi-and-i">James VI and I</a>, in 1613 tied German <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestantism">Protestantism</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a>, and through their daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sophia-of-hanover">Sophia</a>, electress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanover">Hanover</a>, the British throne passed to the house of Hanover in 1714. Frederick V's Bohemian venture turned the revolt of the Bohemian estates into a wider German war, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>, and the transfer of his electoral title to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> in 1623 and the creation of an eighth electorate for his son in 1648 reshaped the Empire's constitution. After the plunder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a> much of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bibliotheca-palatina">Bibliotheca Palatina</a> was given to the pope in 1623, and it remains in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vatican-library">Vatican Library</a>. In the eighteenth century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-theodore-elector-of-bavaria">Charles Theodore</a>'s court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mannheim">Mannheim</a>, with its celebrated orchestra and its Academy of Sciences founded in 1763, was a leading centre of music, where the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolfgang-amadeus-mozart">Mozart</a> sought employment in 1777–1778.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/french-revolutionary-wars"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/french-revolutionary-wars.jpg" alt="French Revolutionary Wars" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/french-revolutionary-wars">French Revolutionary Wars</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:French_Revolutionary_Wars.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ruedi33a, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Palatinate never fully recovered from the seventeenth century. Charles I Louis regained the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palatinate-region">Lower Palatinate</a> in 1648, but the Upper Palatinate stayed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> and the restored electorate ranked last among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a>. The extinction of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simmern">Simmern</a> line in 1685 brought the Catholic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neuburg-an-der-donau">Neuburg</a> branch to power, provoking French claims, the destruction of 1689 and decades of religious conflict with the Protestant majority. In 1777, when the Bavarian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wittelsbach">Wittelsbachs</a> died out, Elector <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-theodore-elector-of-bavaria">Charles Theodore</a> inherited Bavaria, moved his court to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> and merged the two electorates; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>'s attempt to take part of Bavaria led to the brief War of the Bavarian Succession (1778–1779). <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mannheim">Mannheim</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a> became provincial towns. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolutionary-wars">French Revolutionary Wars</a> ended the Palatinate as a state. French armies occupied the left bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> from 1794, and its annexation by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> was recognised in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-campo-formio">Treaty of Campo Formio</a> in 1797 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-luneville">Peace of Lunéville</a> in 1801. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichsdeputationshauptschluss">Imperial Recess</a> (<em>Reichsdeputationshauptschluss</em>) of 1803 the right-bank lands, including Heidelberg and Mannheim, went mainly to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a>, with smaller parts to Hesse-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darmstadt">Darmstadt</a>, the princes of Leiningen and others, while Elector <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximilian-i-joseph-of-bavaria">Maximilian IV Joseph</a> was compensated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconia">Franconia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabia-bavaria">Swabia</a>. From 1816 the left-bank lands belonged to Bavaria again as the Rhenish Palatinate, the origin of the modern name Pfalz.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Electoral Palatinate, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland">Rhineland</a> electorate of the Wittelsbach counts palatine, founded the oldest university in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, gave Reformed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestantism">Protestantism</a> its most widely used catechism and, through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-v-of-the-palatinate">Frederick V</a>'s Bohemian venture, helped set off the Thirty Years' War, before French conquest and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichsdeputationshauptschluss">Imperial Recess of 1803</a> divided it.</em></p>

